⚙️ System Calls



📌 Introduction

User programs cannot directly access hardware or critical OS resources because it may lead to instability and security issues.
To ensure safe interaction, the Operating System provides System Calls — controlled interfaces that allow user programs to request services from the kernel.

👉 System calls act as a gateway between User Mode and Kernel Mode.

🔹 What are System Calls?

System Calls are:

A way for programs to interact with the operating system

A mechanism to access OS services safely

The only legal entry points into the kernel, executed in kernel mode

🧠 Why System Calls are Needed?

Without system calls:

Programs would access hardware directly ❌

System would become insecure and unstable

No standard method to access OS services

System calls ensure:

Security

Stability

Consistency

🧪 Examples of System Calls

Opening a file in C:
fopen() internally uses system calls like open()

Running a program in Linux:
Uses fork() and exec() system calls

Printing output to screen:
Uses the write() system call

🔄 How System Calls Work

User program executes a system call instruction
(e.g., syscall, int 0x80)

CPU switches from User Mode → Kernel Mode

Kernel identifies the system call number

Kernel performs the requested operation
(file access, process creation, memory allocation, etc.)

Kernel switches back to User Mode

Result (success / failure / data) is returned to the program

⚠️ Important Note

System calls do NOT always cause context switching

They mainly cause a mode switch

A context switch happens only if the process is blocked

📂 Types of System Calls

System calls are classified based on the services they provide:

1️⃣ File System

Create, open, read, write, delete files

Manage directories and file permissions

2️⃣ Process Control

Create and terminate processes

Execute programs

Synchronize processes

3️⃣ Memory Management

Allocate and deallocate memory

Manage address spaces

4️⃣ Interprocess Communication (IPC)

Communication between processes

Data sharing and synchronization

5️⃣ Device Management

Request and release devices

Perform read/write operations on devices
